<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Samnite League</h1><p class="st-meta">600 BCE – 290 BCE (approx.) · capital Bovianum</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Confederation of the Oscan-speaking peoples of the southern Apennines, Rome's toughest opponent in Italy during three Samnite Wars.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Samnites lived in the mountains of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/molise">Molise</a> and inland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>. They believed they descended from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sabines">Sabines</a> who had migrated south in a 'sacred spring'. By the 5th century BCE they had formed a confederation of four peoples, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pentri">Pentri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caraceni">Caraceni</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caudini">Caudini</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hirpini">Hirpini</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bovianum">Bovianum</a> as a main centre.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each people (touto) was governed by an elected magistrate, the meddix tuticus, and the league acted together in war. Society was rural, living in villages and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hill-fort">hill forts</a>. The great sanctuary at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietrabbondante">Pietrabbondante</a>, with its temple and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, was a federal centre. Samnite warriors were famed for their armour, and their expansion carried <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oscan-language">Oscan</a> speech into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/campania">Campania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucania">Lucania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bruttium">Bruttium</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> fought three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samnite-wars">Samnite Wars</a> from 343 to 290 BCE. The Samnites trapped a Roman army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-caudine-forks">Caudine Forks</a> in 321 BCE but lost at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sentinum">Sentinum</a> in 295 BCE and accepted Roman alliance in 290 BCE. They later backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hannibal">Hannibal</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> crushed them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-colline-gate">Colline Gate</a> in 82 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/samnite-league.jpg" alt="Samnite League" width="320" height="187"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sc%C3%A8ne_du_retour_du_guerrier_-_Carafa_di_Noja,_Nola_-_Mus%C3%A9e_arch%C3%A9ologique_national_de_Naples_-_inv_9364.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 BCE – 290 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bovianum</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gaius Pontius, Gellius Egnatius</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Oscan polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Oscan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Italy</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Samnites, Samnium</dd></dl></aside></div>

